From: Nura rc4p series <rc4p@nura.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] media: qcom: camss: csid: recover from a reset that times out

A OnePlus 7 Pro (SM8150) reports "CSID reset timeout" on some camera
starts: the first start after a boot once in a while, and every start
after the camera app was closed in the middle of a stream, until the
phone is rebooted.  The next start then fails with -EIO out of
v4l2_pipeline_pm_get() and userspace reports an I/O error.

Three things in the driver turn that single failure into a permanent
one.

csid_set_power() takes the parent (VFE/IFE) reference before it touches
the CSID, but none of its error paths gives it back.  The media core
does not unwind the failing entity either (pipeline_pm_power_one() in
v4l2-mc.c returns before the use count is taken), so one failed start
leaves vfe->power_count at 1 for good: the IFE's power domain keeps its
runtime-PM-active device link, the VFE's clocks stay enabled and the
CAMSS top domain never suspends again.  A CSID left in a bad state by
the failed reset is therefore never power cycled, which is why only a
reboot clears it: the retry a user does by hand hits the same state.

The CSID is also powered down with its channel still enabled.
csid_set_stream() writes the stream configuration only when
need_vc_update is set, and that flag is cleared on the way into
streaming, so the halt/disable write never happens on the way out.  The
RDI keeps consuming data while the clocks are cut, and the block comes
back up mid-frame; RST_STROBES resets the block with its registers
preserved, so that state is still there when the next reset runs.  That
matches the failures right after a stream is cut short, and it is the
likeliest way to get the block into a state where reset done never
arrives.

Finally, csid_reset() waits only for the reset-done interrupt.  If the
edge is lost (an earlier edge still pending in the GIC, or a pulse
raised while the block's clock was settling after the power up), a
reset that did complete is reported as a timeout.

So: unwind csid_power_on() through one error path that always drops the
parent reference, halt the channel in csid_set_stream(0) and write the
configuration again on the next start, poll the reset-done status bit
alongside the completion, and, when the reset still times out, power
cycle the CSID once and reset again instead of failing the camera.  The
timeout message keeps its wording, so existing log greps still match,
and now prints the top and receive interrupt status and RDI0's
configuration.

With the references balanced, a start that follows a failed one begins
from a block that has actually been power cycled, so a retry no longer
inherits the broken state - that is what turns "dead until reboot" into
"one bad start".  The in-driver retry covers the other failure mode, a
reset pulse or its interrupt edge that never reached the block: the
second reset runs against clocks that are already running and rated.

On the phone the failure is reproducible by closing the camera app
mid-stream and reopening it.  Compile tested only: the module builds
with W=1 and no warnings, on the v7.0.0-sm8150 tree with 0028-0031
applied.
---

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csid.c b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csid.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csid.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csid.c
@@ -675,75 +675,112 @@
  *
  * Return 0 on success or a negative error code otherwise
  */
-static int csid_set_power(struct v4l2_subdev *sd, int on)
+/*
+ * csid_power_off - Power down the CSID module and drop its references
+ * @csid: CSID device
+ *
+ * The parent (VFE/IFE) reference goes too: it is what holds the IFE's power
+ * domain up, and one left behind here would keep the CSID from ever being
+ * power cycled again, so a block stuck in a failed reset would stay stuck
+ * until the next boot.
+ */
+static void csid_power_off(struct csid_device *csid)
+{
+	disable_irq(csid->irq);
+	camss_disable_clocks(csid->nclocks, csid->clock);
+	regulator_bulk_disable(csid->num_supplies, csid->supplies);
+	pm_runtime_put_sync(csid->camss->dev);
+	csid->res->parent_dev_ops->put(csid->camss, csid->id);
+}
+
+static int csid_power_on(struct csid_device *csid)
 {
-	struct csid_device *csid = v4l2_get_subdevdata(sd);
 	struct camss *camss = csid->camss;
 	struct device *dev = camss->dev;
-	int ret = 0;
+	int ret;
 
-	if (on) {
-		/*
-		 * From SDM845 onwards, the VFE needs to be powered on before
-		 * switching on the CSID. Do so unconditionally, as there is no
-		 * drawback in following the same powering order on older SoCs.
-		 */
-		ret = csid->res->parent_dev_ops->get(camss, csid->id);
-		if (ret < 0)
-			return ret;
-
-		ret = pm_runtime_resume_and_get(dev);
-		if (ret < 0)
-			return ret;
-
-		ret = regulator_bulk_enable(csid->num_supplies,
-					    csid->supplies);
-		if (ret < 0) {
-			pm_runtime_put_sync(dev);
-			return ret;
-		}
+	/*
+	 * From SDM845 onwards, the VFE needs to be powered on before switching
+	 * on the CSID. Do so unconditionally, as there is no drawback in
+	 * following the same powering order on older SoCs.
+	 */
+	ret = csid->res->parent_dev_ops->get(camss, csid->id);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+
+	ret = pm_runtime_resume_and_get(dev);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		goto error_parent;
+
+	ret = regulator_bulk_enable(csid->num_supplies, csid->supplies);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		goto error_pm_runtime;
+
+	ret = csid_set_clock_rates(csid);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		goto error_regulators;
+
+	ret = camss_enable_clocks(csid->nclocks, csid->clock, dev);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		goto error_regulators;
+
+	csid->phy.need_vc_update = true;
+
+	enable_irq(csid->irq);
+
+	ret = csid->res->hw_ops->reset(csid);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		goto error_clocks;
 
-		ret = csid_set_clock_rates(csid);
-		if (ret < 0) {
-			regulator_bulk_disable(csid->num_supplies,
-					       csid->supplies);
-			pm_runtime_put_sync(dev);
-			return ret;
-		}
+	csid->res->hw_ops->hw_version(csid);
 
-		ret = camss_enable_clocks(csid->nclocks, csid->clock, dev);
-		if (ret < 0) {
-			regulator_bulk_disable(csid->num_supplies,
-					       csid->supplies);
-			pm_runtime_put_sync(dev);
-			return ret;
-		}
+	return 0;
 
-		csid->phy.need_vc_update = true;
+error_clocks:
+	disable_irq(csid->irq);
+	camss_disable_clocks(csid->nclocks, csid->clock);
+error_regulators:
+	regulator_bulk_disable(csid->num_supplies, csid->supplies);
+error_pm_runtime:
+	pm_runtime_put_sync(dev);
+error_parent:
+	csid->res->parent_dev_ops->put(camss, csid->id);
 
-		enable_irq(csid->irq);
+	return ret;
+}
 
-		ret = csid->res->hw_ops->reset(csid);
-		if (ret < 0) {
-			disable_irq(csid->irq);
-			camss_disable_clocks(csid->nclocks, csid->clock);
-			regulator_bulk_disable(csid->num_supplies,
-					       csid->supplies);
-			pm_runtime_put_sync(dev);
-			return ret;
-		}
+/*
+ * csid_set_power - Power on/off CSID module
+ * @sd: CSID V4L2 subdevice
+ * @on: Requested power state
+ *
+ * A reset that never reports done leaves the block in whatever state it came
+ * up in, and nothing but a power cycle clears that: the first start after a
+ * boot can hit it once, and a start after a stream was cut short can hit it
+ * every time. Drop the block and try once more from cold instead of failing
+ * the camera until the next reboot.
+ *
+ * Return 0 on success or a negative error code otherwise
+ */
+static int csid_set_power(struct v4l2_subdev *sd, int on)
+{
+	struct csid_device *csid = v4l2_get_subdevdata(sd);
+	int ret;
 
-		csid->res->hw_ops->hw_version(csid);
-	} else {
-		disable_irq(csid->irq);
-		camss_disable_clocks(csid->nclocks, csid->clock);
-		regulator_bulk_disable(csid->num_supplies,
-				       csid->supplies);
-		pm_runtime_put_sync(dev);
-		csid->res->parent_dev_ops->put(camss, csid->id);
+	if (!on) {
+		csid_power_off(csid);
+		return 0;
 	}
 
-	return ret;
+	ret = csid_power_on(csid);
+	if (ret != -EIO)
+		return ret;
+
+	dev_warn(csid->camss->dev,
+		 "CSID%u: power cycle and reset again after the timeout\n",
+		 csid->id);
+
+	return csid_power_on(csid);
 }
 
 /*
@@ -773,11 +810,21 @@
 		if (!csid->testgen.enabled &&
 		    !media_pad_remote_pad_first(&csid->pads[MSM_CSID_PAD_SINK]))
 			return -ENOLINK;
-	}
 
-	if (csid->phy.need_vc_update) {
-		csid->res->hw_ops->configure_stream(csid, enable);
-		csid->phy.need_vc_update = false;
+		if (csid->phy.need_vc_update) {
+			csid->res->hw_ops->configure_stream(csid, enable);
+			csid->phy.need_vc_update = false;
+		}
+	} else {
+		/*
+		 * Halt the channel and disable it while the clocks still run,
+		 * and write the configuration again on the next start. Left
+		 * enabled across the power down, the CSID's receive logic sits
+		 * mid-frame, where the next reset can wait for a frame
+		 * boundary that never comes.
+		 */
+		csid->res->hw_ops->configure_stream(csid, 0);
+		csid->phy.need_vc_update = true;
 	}
 
 	return 0;
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csid-gen2.c b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csid-gen2.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csid-gen2.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csid-gen2.c
@@ -394,6 +394,7 @@
 {
 	unsigned long time;
 	u32 val;
+	int i;
 
 	reinit_completion(&csid->reset_complete);
 
@@ -406,14 +407,35 @@
 	val = 0x1e << RST_STROBES;
 	writel_relaxed(val, csid->base + CSID_RST_STROBES);
 
-	time = wait_for_completion_timeout(&csid->reset_complete,
-					   msecs_to_jiffies(CSID_RESET_TIMEOUT_MS));
-	if (!time) {
-		dev_err(csid->camss->dev, "CSID reset timeout\n");
-		return -EIO;
+	/*
+	 * Reset done is reported by an interrupt, but an edge can be lost: a
+	 * masked interrupt still pending in the GIC swallows the next one, and
+	 * a pulse raised while the block's clock was settling after the power
+	 * up leaves nothing to deliver. Read the status bit as well, so that
+	 * a finished reset is never mistaken for a stuck one.
+	 */
+	for (i = 0; i < CSID_RESET_TIMEOUT_MS / 10; i++) {
+		time = wait_for_completion_timeout(&csid->reset_complete,
+						   msecs_to_jiffies(10));
+		if (time)
+			return 0;
+
+		if (readl_relaxed(csid->base + CSID_TOP_IRQ_STATUS) &
+		    BIT(TOP_IRQ_STATUS_RESET_DONE)) {
+			dev_dbg(csid->camss->dev,
+				"CSID%u: reset done without an interrupt\n",
+				csid->id);
+			return 0;
+		}
 	}
 
-	return 0;
+	dev_err(csid->camss->dev,
+		"CSID reset timeout: top irq %#x, rx irq %#x, rdi0 %#x\n",
+		readl_relaxed(csid->base + CSID_TOP_IRQ_STATUS),
+		readl_relaxed(csid->base + CSID_CSI2_RX_IRQ_STATUS),
+		readl_relaxed(csid->base + CSID_RDI_CFG0(0)));
+
+	return -EIO;
 }
 
 static void csid_subdev_init(struct csid_device *csid)
